e conclusion drawn from looking at the screen is void.1781:  *1782:  *   The BIST probe in this file enables it on EVERY boot and turns it off about1783:  *   four seconds later -- and the turn-off was written as a bare1784:  *  1785:  *       kickpi_k7_mipi_dsi_write2(dev, 0x2f, 0x00);1786:  *1787:  *   with the return value discarded, no read-back, and FRM_PT (0x2C/0x2D/0x2E)1788:  *   LEFT AT 0xFF -- i.e. every pattern still armed.  So there was no evidence1789:  *   whatsoever that the panel ever left BIST mode, and if it did not, the panel1790:  *   has been free-running a pattern that looks like "a regular white/black test1791:  *   matrix" over the top of the entire bring-up.  The BIST probe is now1792:  *   disabled; this function exists so that state cannot survive anyway.1793:  *1794:  *   It clears the pattern selection AND the enable, then READS THE ENABLE BACK1795:  *   and reports it, because an unchecked write is exactly how the previous1796:  *   version came to be trusted.1797:  *1798:  * Returned Value:1799:  *   OK if the enable reads back clear, -EIO otherwise.1800:  *1801:  ****************************************************************************/1802: 1803: static int kickpi_k7_mipi_dsi_bist_clear(FAR struct mipi_dsi_device *dev)1804: {1805:   static const uint8_t page4[4] = { 0xff, 0x98, 0x81, 0x04 };yunline@ovela-dev:~/projects/ovela-dev-workspace/contest2026_062_PharosTech$
